NNAAMMEE
     aauuddiittddiissttdd..ccoonnff — configuration file for the auditdistd(8) daemon.

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     Note: the configuration file may contain passwords.  Care should be taken
     to configure proper permissions for this file (e.g., 0600).

     Every line starting with # gets treated as a comment and is ignored.

CCOONNFFIIGGUURRAATTIIOONN FFIILLEE SSYYNNTTAAXX
     The general syntax of the aauuddiittddiissttdd..ccoonnff file is as follows:

     ## Global section.

     # Our name.
     # The default is the first part of the hostname.
     name "<name>"

     # Connection timeout.
     # The default is 5.
     timeout <seconds>

     # Path to pidfile.
     # The default is "/var/run/auditdistd.pid".
     pidfile "<path>"

     sender {
             ## Sender section.

             # Source address for connections.
             # Optional.
             source "<addr>"

             # Directory with audit trail files managed by auditdistd.
             # The default is /var/audit/dist.
             directory "<dir>"

             # Configuration for the target system we want to send audit trail
             # files to.
             host "<name>" {
                     # Source address for connections.
                     # Optional.
                     source "<addr>"

                     # Address of the auditdistd receiver.
                     # No default. Obligatory.
                     remote "<addr>"

                     # Directory with audit trail files managed by auditdistd.
                     # The default is /var/audit/dist.
                     directory "<dir>"

                     # Fingerprint of the receiver's public key when using TLS
                     # for connections.
                     # Example fingerprint:
                     # SHA256=8F:0A:FC:8A:3D:09:80:AF:D9:AA:38:CC:8A:86:53:E6:8F:B6:1C:55:30:14:D7:F9:AA:8B:3E:73:CD:F5:76:2B
                     fingerprint "<algorithm=hash>"

                     # Password used to authenticate in front of the receiver.
                     password "<password>"
             }

             # Currently local audit trail files can be sent only to one remote
             # auditdistd receiver, but this can change in the future.
     }

     receiver {
             ## Receiver section.

             # Address to listen on. Multiple listen addresses may be specified.
             # The defaults are "tcp4://0.0.0.0:7878" and "tcp6://[::]:7878".
             listen "<addr>"

             # Base directory.
             # If the directory in the host section is not absolute, it will be
             # concatenated with this base directory.
             # The default is "/var/audit/remote".
             directory "<basedir>"

             # Path to the receiver's certificate file.
             # The default is "/etc/security/auditdistd.cert.pem".
             certfile "<path>"

             # Path to the receiver's private key file.
             # The default is "/etc/security/auditdistd.key.pem".
             keyfile "<path>"

             # Configuration for a source system we want to receive audit trail
             # files from.
             host "<name>" {
                     # Sender address.
                     # No default. Obligatory.
                     remote "<addr>"

                     # Directory where to store audit trail files received
                     # from system <name>.
                     # The default is "<basedir>/<name>".
                     directory "<dir>"

                     # Password used by the sender to authenticate.
                     password "<password>"
             }

             # Multiple hosts to receive from can be configured.
     }

     Most of the various available configuration parameters are optional.  If
     a parameter is not defined in the particular section, it will be inher‐
     ited from the parent section if possible.  For example, if the ssoouurrccee pa‐
     rameter is not defined in the hhoosstt section, it will be inherited from the
     sseennddeerr section.  In case the gglloobbaall section does not define the ssoouurrccee
     parameter at all, the default value will be used.

CCOONNFFIIGGUURRAATTIIOONN OOPPTTIIOONN DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The following statements are available:

     nnaammee ⟨name⟩

           This host's name.  It is sent to the receiver, so it can properly
           recognize us if there are multiple senders coming from the same IP
           address.

     ttiimmeeoouutt ⟨seconds⟩

           Connection timeout in seconds.  The default value is _5.

     ppiiddffiillee ⟨path⟩

           File in which to store the process ID of the main auditdistd(8)
           process.

           The default value is _/_v_a_r_/_r_u_n_/_a_u_d_i_t_d_i_s_t_d_._p_i_d.

     ssoouurrccee ⟨addr⟩

           Local address to bind to before connecting to the remote aauuddiittddiissttdd
           daemon.  The format is the same as for the lliisstteenn statement.

     ddiirreeccttoorryy ⟨path⟩

           The directory where to look for audit trail files in case of sender
           mode, or the directory where to store received audit trail files.
           The provided path has to be an absolute path.  The only exception
           is when the directory is provided in the rreecceeiivveerr section; then the
           path provided in the hhoosstt subsections can be relative to the direc‐
           tory in the rreecceeiivveerr section.  The default value is _/_v_a_r_/_a_u_d_i_t_/_d_i_s_t
           for the entire sseennddeerr section, _/_v_a_r_/_a_u_d_i_t_/_r_e_m_o_t_e for the non-host
           rreecceeiivveerr section and _/_v_a_r_/_a_u_d_i_t_/_r_e_m_o_t_e_/_<_n_a_m_e_> for the hhoosstt subsec‐
           tions in the rreecceeiivveerr section where ⟨name⟩ is the host's name.

     rreemmoottee ⟨addr⟩

           Address of the remote aauuddiittddiissttdd daemon.  The format is the same as
           for the lliisstteenn statement.  When operating in sseennddeerr mode this ad‐
           dress will be used to connect to the rreecceeiivveerr.  When operating in
           rreecceeiivveerr mode only connections from this address will be accepted.

     lliisstteenn ⟨addr⟩

           Address to listen on in form of:

                 protocol://protocol-specific-address

           Each of the following examples defines the same listen address:

                 0.0.0.0
                 0.0.0.0:7878
                 tcp://0.0.0.0
                 tcp://0.0.0.0:7878
                 tcp4://0.0.0.0
                 tcp4://0.0.0.0:7878

           Multiple listen addresses can be specified.  By default aauuddiittddiissttdd
           listens on _t_c_p_4_:_/_/_0_._0_._0_._0_:_7_8_7_8 and _t_c_p_6_:_/_/_[_:_:_]_:_7_8_7_8, if the kernel
           supports IPv4 and IPv6 respectively.

     kkeeyyffiillee ⟨path⟩

           Path to a file that contains the private key for TLS communication.

     cceerrttffiillee ⟨path⟩

           Path to a file that contains the certificate for TLS communication.

     ffiinnggeerrpprriinntt ⟨algo=hash⟩

           Fingerprint of the receiver's public key.  Currently only the
           SHA256 algorithm is supported.  The certificate public key's fin‐
           gerprint ready to be pasted into the aauuddiittddiissttdd configuration file
           can be obtained by running:

           # openssl x509 -in /etc/security/auditdistd.cert.pem -noout -fingerprint -sha256 | awk -F '[ =]' '{printf("%s=%s\n", $1, $3)}'

     ppaasssswwoorrdd ⟨password⟩

           Password used to authenticate the sender in front of the receiver.

FFIILLEESS
     /etc/security/auditdistd.conf  The default aauuddiittddiissttdd configuration file.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     The example configuration files can look as follows.

     Web server:

           sender {
                   host backup {
                           remote 10.0.0.4
                   }
           }

     Audit backup server:

           receiver {
                   host webserv {
                           remote 10.0.0.1
                   }
                   host mailserv {
                           remote 10.0.0.2
                   }
                   host dnsserv {
                           remote 10.0.0.3
                   }
           }

